# .copytreeignore — what `copytree` should NOT include when packaging this
# project as context for an AI agent. gitignore-style syntax, evaluated after
# .gitignore.
#
# Goal: a PURE-CODE bundle. Everything needed to understand and change the
# server — source, server-owned prompts, skill bodies, contracts, docs, deploy
# and CI config — and nothing that is an environment, a cache, a build
# artifact, a captured fixture, a model weight, or a second copy of a
# different repository.
#
# .gitignore already removes the environments, caches, build artifacts and
# real .env files, and CopyTree drops binaries and junk on its own, so this
# file deliberately carries ONLY the rules that go further than those two.
# Keep it that way: `copytree ignore check .` flags any rule here that has
# stopped doing work of its own.
#
#   copytree inspect .        what would be selected, by directory
#   copytree ignore check .   what these rules remove, and which are dead
#   copytree plan .           the exact final export
#
# Escape hatches — `--force-include` overrides these rules (a CLI `-x` does
# not), so nothing here is permanently out of reach:
#   copytree . --force-include 'tests/**'         # + the test suite
#   copytree . --force-include 'benchmarks/**'    # + the benchmark harness
#   copytree . --force-include 'cli/**'           # + the vendored Go CLI
#
# Recommended invocation for this repo:
#
#   copytree . --secrets off
#
# The default `--secrets redact` scanner has no true positives here — nothing
# tracked in this repo is a credential — but 15 false positives, and it
# rewrites the exact lines an agent most needs to read: `token =
# _extract_bearer(authorization)` in auth.py, every `api_key=` argument
# threading the caller's key through providers/, and every `max_tokens=`.
# A bundle that mangles the credential path is worse than no bundle. Turning
# the policy off also restores .env.example (8 KB documenting every setting),
# which the secret-FILE rule drops and --force-include cannot override.
#
# That is only safe because the real .env can never reach the export: it is
# gitignored, and the rule at the foot of this file states it again so the
# guarantee does not rest on the secret policy that is being switched off.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Vendored CLI submodule (~1.5 MB of Go, a quarter of the old export)
#
# cli/ is a git submodule tracking the separate Daintree CLI repo. It is not
# part of this Python package, build, or test scope, and the server/client
# boundary it implements is already described prose-first in CLAUDE.md and
# specified in docs/DAINTREE_API.md. Walking it here means an agent burns its
# context on a codebase it was not asked to change. Note it carries its own
# .claude/ and .github/, which is why those rules appear twice in a check.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
cli/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Training corpus and exported model artifacts
#
# training/ keeps its CODE — the local selector's pipeline is part of this
# system, and EXPERIMENTS.md records which designs were already built and
# abandoned, which is the single most expensive thing for an agent to
# rediscover. What goes is the corpus and what it produces: the assembled
# JSONL, the 40 KB catalog digest, and the exported vector table the server
# loads at runtime (189 KB of base64 that reads as noise). Checkpoints and
# sweep results under training/models/ and training/results/ are gitignored
# already; the wildcards below are what catches a stray export anywhere else.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
training/data/
src/daintree_assistant_server/skills/local/artifacts/
*.npz
*.onnx
*.jsonl

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Captured fixtures (generated, not authored)
#
# A 91 KB dump of the CLI's `Registry.OpenAITools` — JSON schema restating
# what cli/ already declares, refreshed by hand from the submodule. The
# regeneration recipe lives beside the file for whoever needs it. Its sibling
# projection, tests/fixtures/cli_tool_names.txt, goes with the test suite.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
benchmarks/_harness/fixtures/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Test suite (~1.2 MB, larger than the source it covers)
#
# Excluded by default, not because it is unimportant but because it is
# derivative: the contracts it pins are stated in src/ and docs/, and a single
# module here runs to 94 KB. Pull it back with `--force-include 'tests/**'`
# when the task is actually about the tests.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
tests/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Benchmark harness (~670 KB)
#
# The READMEs are kept — each states what its suite measures and what it
# gates, which is the part that explains the system. Runners, scoring code and
# the YAML case corpora are harness implementation detail. Excluded by
# extension rather than by directory so the READMEs survive the rule.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
benchmarks/**/*.py
benchmarks/**/*.yaml
benchmarks/**/*.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Claude Code tooling + git worktrees (huge; pure duplication)
#
# .claude/worktrees/<name>/ is a full second checkout of this repo created by
# `git worktree`. It is gitignored, but because each worktree is its own git
# boundary, copytree re-evaluates ignore rules *inside* it (where everything
# is tracked) and walks the entire duplicate. None of .claude/ (local
# settings, worktrees, session state) belongs in a bundle of the project.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.claude/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Real environment files
#
# Deliberately duplicates .gitignore, so `copytree ignore check .` will call
# this rule redundant. Keep it: the recommended invocation above disables the
# secret policy, and this is what makes that safe without depending on a
# second file staying correct.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
